## In 30 seconds

Attach **Securable** behavior data to a Model; a registered **SecurityBehavior** consults **SecurityService** at init and adjusts visibility or disable state. The default `SecurityBehaviorImpl` is a stub — register your own `@Register(SecurityBehavior.class)` implementation for real policy.

Quick Start
---------------------

Add the security module and attach a `Securable` behavior data to any Model. A `SecurityBehavior` implementation consults your `SecurityService` and applies the result to the UI.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>security</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

**Minimal example — hide a panel when the user lacks permission:**

```java
public class AdminDashboardModel extends DefaultModel<AdminDashboardModel, AdminDashboardView> {

    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        addBehavior(Securable.create().name("admin.dashboard"));
    }
}

@Register(SecurityService.class)
public class MySecurityService extends AbstractService implements SecurityService {
    // connect to your auth backend; return grants for current user
}

@Register(value = SecurityBehavior.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Normal)
public class MySecurityBehavior extends AbstractBehavior<Securable, Model>
        implements SecurityBehavior {

    @Override
    public void initBehavior() {
        Securable data = getBehaviorData();
        SecurityService security = getService(SecurityService.class);
        boolean allowed = /* check data.name() against security */;
        node().setVisible(allowed);
        node().setDisable(!allowed);
    }
}
```

Replace the placeholder authorization check with your real policy. The default `SecurityBehaviorImpl` is a no-op stub you override via `@Register`.


Security
==============


Overview
---------------------

The JRebirth Security module provides a **Behavior-based** approach to securing UI components. Rather than embedding security logic directly in Models or Views, you attach a `SecurityBehavior` to any `BehavioredComponent`. The behavior inspects the associated `Securable` data and interacts with a `SecurityService` to determine whether the component should be shown/hidden, enabled/disabled, or otherwise restricted.

This design keeps security concerns decoupled from business logic and allows consistent access-control policies across the entire application.


Architecture
---------------------

The security module follows a three-tier pattern:

1. **SecurityBehavior** &mdash; the pluggable behavior attached to a Model.
2. **Securable** &mdash; the BehaviorData carrying security metadata (permission name, roles, etc.).
3. **SecurityService** &mdash; the service contract for authentication and authorization decisions.

The typical flow:

```java
// 1) Model declares what to protect
@Override
protected void initModel() {
    addBehavior(Securable.create().name("admin.dashboard"));
}

// 2) Behavior enforces policy at init
@Override
public void initBehavior() {
    Securable data = getBehaviorData();
    SecurityService auth = getService(SecurityService.class);
    boolean allowed = auth.isGranted(data.name()); // method on your SecurityService impl
    node().setVisible(allowed);
    node().setDisable(!allowed);
}
```


SecurityBehavior
---------------------

The **SecurityBehavior** interface extends `Behavior<Securable, Model>` and is annotated with `@RegistrationPoint(exclusive = true)`, meaning only one security behavior implementation can be active at a time.


```java
@RegistrationPoint(exclusive = true)
public interface SecurityBehavior extends Behavior<Securable, Model> {

}
```


SecurityBehaviorImpl
---------------------

**SecurityBehaviorImpl** is the default implementation, extending `AbstractBehavior<Securable, Model>`. Override `initBehavior()` to query `SecurityService` and show/hide or enable/disable the model's node.


```java
public class SecurityBehaviorImpl extends AbstractBehavior<Securable, Model>
        implements SecurityBehavior {

    @Override
    public void initBehavior() {
        // Default stub — no UI changes; override via @Register
    }
}
```

Register a custom behavior to replace the stub:

```java
@Register(value = SecurityBehavior.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Normal)
public class AppSecurityBehavior extends AbstractBehavior<Securable, Model>
        implements SecurityBehavior {

    @Override
    public void initBehavior() {
        Securable data = getBehaviorData();
        boolean ok = getService(SecurityService.class).isGranted(data.name());
        node().setVisible(ok);
        node().setDisable(!ok);
    }
}
```


Securable
---------------------

**Securable** is the BehaviorData class annotated with `@BehaviorDataFor(SecurityBehavior.class)`. It carries security configuration for the component it is attached to. The `name` property identifies the permission or resource being secured.

Use the fluent builder pattern to create instances:

```java
Securable.create().name("admin.dashboard")
```


```java
@BehaviorDataFor(SecurityBehavior.class)
public class Securable extends BehaviorDataBase implements Serializable {

    /** The generated serial version uid. */
    private static final long serialVersionUID = -2309831247976039337L;

    /** . */
    private String name;

    public static Securable create() {
        return new Securable();
    }

    public String name() {
        return this.name;
    }

    public Securable name(final String name) {
        this.name = name;
        return this;
    }

}
```


SecurityService
---------------------

**SecurityService** extends the JRebirth `Service` interface and serves as the contract for authentication and authorization logic. Implement this interface to connect to your security backend (LDAP, OAuth, custom token service, etc.).


```java
public interface SecurityService extends Service {
    // Add your auth API (e.g. boolean isGranted(String key))
}
```

Implement and register your backend connector:

```java
@Register(SecurityService.class)
public class LdapSecurityService extends AbstractService implements SecurityService {
    public boolean isGranted(String permissionKey) {
        return currentUser().hasPermission(permissionKey);
    }
}
```

Usage Pattern
---------------------

### Attaching Security to a Model ###

In your Model's initialization, add a `Securable` data instance:

```java
addBehavior(Securable.create().name("my.permission.key"));
```

This triggers the `SecurityBehavior` to be created and attached. The behavior then:

1. Retrieves the `Securable` data.
2. Calls the `SecurityService` to check if the current user has the required permission.
3. Applies the result to the UI (show/hide, enable/disable).

### Implementing SecurityService ###

Create a class implementing `SecurityService` and register it:

```java
@Register(SecurityService.class)
public class MySecurityService extends AbstractService implements SecurityService {
    // Implement authentication/authorization logic
}
```

### Replacing the Default Behavior ###

Since `SecurityBehavior` uses `@RegistrationPoint(exclusive = true)`, registering your own implementation with a higher priority will replace the default:

```java
@Register(value = SecurityBehavior.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Normal)
public class CustomSecurityBehavior extends AbstractBehavior<Securable, Model>
        implements SecurityBehavior {
    // Custom security enforcement
}
```


